        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The Radiation Monitoring Container Device (RMCD) studied the physical properties and biological effects of radiation levels inside the Shuttle. The experiment consisted of layers of radiation detectors and biological specimens (bacterial spores, Bacillus subtilis; shrimp eggs, Altemia salina; and maize seeds, Zea mays) sandwiched together.
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
The detectors, which consisted of sheets of plastic materials (TS-16 and CR-39), recorded the individual nuclear tracks of cosmic radiation while the dosimeter package, which contained conventional detectors made of materials such as lithium flouride or magnesium-silica-terbium, accumulated radiation energy. The experiment was mounted in the aft end cone of the Spacelab and the biological specimens were exposed to cosmic radiation for approximately 6 days during the mission.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
The specimens were examined by biological and biochemical methods to determine the effects of radiation on the enclosed organisms. The results of this investigation were used in developing a sensitive solid-state nuclear detector for future space flights and to improve basic understanding of radiation biology.
<p>
        </div>

